import{_ as t,o as s,c as o,ag as a}from"./chunks/framework.2cFhd8Ux.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"API Keys, Tokens and Spending Caps","description":"","frontmatter":{"source":"setup/api-keys.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"setup/api-keys.md","filePath":"setup/api-keys.md"}'),n={name:"setup/api-keys.md"};function i(r,e,d,l,h,c){return s(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a(`<h1 id="api-keys-tokens-and-spending-caps" tabindex="-1">API Keys, Tokens and Spending Caps <a class="header-anchor" href="#api-keys-tokens-and-spending-caps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;API Keys, Tokens and Spending Caps&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>How to keep keys out of repositories and away from agents, how to scope tokens, and how to switch the kits between providers or the lab machine by configuration alone.</p></blockquote><p>Read this before week 1&#39;s Lab. It applies safety contract rules <a href="./safety-contract.html#4-no-real-secrets-near-an-agent">4</a>, <a href="./safety-contract.html#5-scoped-tokens-only">5</a> and <a href="./safety-contract.html#6-spending-caps-at-zero">6</a>.</p><p><strong>Why it matters more with agents.</strong> A coding agent reads files and runs commands. Anything it can read, a prompt injection can ask it to send somewhere (<a href="./../resources/case-studies.html#prompt-injection-and-agent-incidents">case studies</a>), and any credential it can find, it may use: in one 2026 incident an agent deleted a production volume with a token it found in an unrelated file (<a href="./../resources/case-studies.html#c2-pocketos-a-token-found-in-an-unrelated-file">PocketOS</a>). Stolen AI keys are now a malware target in their own right (<a href="./../resources/case-studies.html#b4-malware-that-hunts-for-ai-keys">supply chain</a>). So the course&#39;s keys are built to be worth nothing if stolen: free tier, no payment method, one purpose each.</p><h2 id="the-four-rules" tabindex="-1">The four rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-four-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The four rules&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>A key never enters a repository</strong>, a chat, an issue, a PR, a log or a screenshot. Not even in a commit you plan to delete: history keeps it.</li><li><strong>Every key is free-tier with no payment method</strong>, so its spending cap is zero. Where a service needs a card, set the lowest hard cap it offers and turn off automatic top-ups. A budget <em>alert</em> is not a cap.</li><li><strong>Every token is scoped</strong> to one job, one repository and the fewest permissions, with an expiry date.</li><li><strong>An agent session that reads untrusted text has no real secret in reach.</strong> The one exception is unavoidable: the agent&#39;s own model key has to be in its environment for it to work at all. That&#39;s why that key must be free-tier with no payment method, worth nothing to steal. Everything else (GitHub tokens with write access, other providers&#39; keys, <code>.env</code> files from other projects) stays out of any session that reads issues, web pages, the class codebase or another team&#39;s code.</li></ol><h2 id="keys-in-env" tabindex="-1">Keys in <code>.env</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#keys-in-env" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Keys in \`.env\`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The kits read three environment variables and speak the OpenAI chat-completions format:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>What it holds</th><th>Secret?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>LLM_BASE_URL</code></td><td>The provider&#39;s OpenAI-compatible base URL</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>LLM_API_KEY</code></td><td>Your key (for Ollama, any non-empty string)</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>LLM_MODEL</code></td><td>The model ID</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In a kit&#39;s folder, create a <code>.env</code> file (most kits ship a <code>.env.example</code> to copy; the kit&#39;s README lists its variables):</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">cp</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> .env.example</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> .env</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> check-ignore</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -v</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> .env</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The second command must print the <code>.gitignore</code> line that ignores <code>.env</code>. If it prints nothing, stop and fix <code>.gitignore</code> before you put a key in the file. Then fill in <code>.env</code> with the values for your provider from <a href="./../TOOLS.html#llm-apis-for-the-kits">Tool Handout: LLM APIs for the kits</a>:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>LLM_BASE_URL=[base URL from TOOLS.md]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>LLM_API_KEY=[your key]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>LLM_MODEL=[model ID from TOOLS.md]</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong>Better still, use a Codespaces secret</strong> for <code>LLM_API_KEY</code>: github.com → Settings → Codespaces → <strong>New secret</strong>, scoped to the one repository that needs it. The key then never exists as a file in the project, so neither <code>git add .</code> nor an agent&#39;s <code>cat .env</code> can expose it. Keep <code>LLM_BASE_URL</code> and <code>LLM_MODEL</code> in <code>.env</code>. Put each variable in one place only. Check it arrived without printing it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">test</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -n</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$LLM_API_KEY</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> &amp;&amp; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;LLM_API_KEY is set&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><div class="warning custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">WARNING</p><p><strong>A Codespaces secret is still visible to an agent</strong> that runs <code>env</code> or <code>printenv</code> in that codespace. The protection is against committing the key, not against an agent. That is why the injection lab (week 4) uses a worthless canary instead of a real key, and why a codespace in which an agent reads untrusted text gets <strong>no</strong> secrets attached (<a href="./sandboxes.html#what-a-sandbox-does-and-doesnt-protect">sandboxes</a>).</p></div><h2 id="switching-provider-or-using-the-lab-machine" tabindex="-1">Switching provider, or using the lab machine <a class="header-anchor" href="#switching-provider-or-using-the-lab-machine" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Switching provider, or using the lab machine&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Because every kit reads the same three variables, switching provider is a configuration change: edit <code>.env</code> (or the Codespaces secret), restart the kit, and nothing in the code changes. You&#39;ll do this on purpose in week 1, and for real whenever a free tier runs dry.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>To use</th><th><code>LLM_BASE_URL</code></th><th><code>LLM_API_KEY</code></th><th><code>LLM_MODEL</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The primary free API</td><td>from <a href="./../TOOLS.html#llm-apis-for-the-kits">Tool Handout: LLM APIs for the kits</a></td><td>your primary key</td><td>the suggested model in TOOLS.md</td></tr><tr><td>The fallback free API</td><td>from TOOLS.md</td><td>your fallback key</td><td>its suggested model</td></tr><tr><td>The lab machine</td><td><code>http://[lab-machine-host]:11434/v1</code> (your instructor gives the host)</td><td><code>ollama</code>, or the class token if the instructor set one</td><td>the lab model in TOOLS.md</td></tr><tr><td>Ollama on your laptop</td><td><code>http://localhost:11434/v1</code></td><td><code>ollama</code></td><td>the laptop model in TOOLS.md</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Keep one file per provider (<code>.env.gemini</code>, <code>.env.groq</code>, <code>.env.lab</code>) and copy the one you want over <code>.env</code>. The kits&#39; <code>.gitignore</code> files ignore <code>.env</code> and <code>.env.*</code> (except <code>.env.example</code>); in any other repository, add both patterns before you create the files, and check with <code>git check-ignore -v .env.groq</code>. Log every switch in your <code>AI_LOG.md</code>: a result produced by a different model is a different result.</p><div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p><strong>Model output differs by provider.</strong> A prompt that works against the primary may fail against a small local model, and a test that passes on recorded responses may fail live. When you switch, re-run the kit&#39;s tests and one real task before you trust the switch.</p></div><h2 id="scoped-tokens" tabindex="-1">Scoped tokens <a class="header-anchor" href="#scoped-tokens" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scoped tokens&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An agent needs a GitHub token when it works with issues or pull requests, and a CI workflow needs one to post results. Give each the least it needs.</p><p><strong>Fine-grained personal access tokens</strong> (github.com → Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens → Fine-grained tokens):</p><ul><li><strong>Repository access:</strong> <em>Only select repositories</em>: one repository, your team fork.</li><li><strong>Permissions:</strong> start with <strong>Contents: Read</strong> and <strong>Metadata: Read</strong>. Add <strong>Pull requests: Write</strong> or <strong>Issues: Write</strong> only when a task needs it, and only for the token used by that task.</li><li><strong>Expiration:</strong> the end of the lab or the milestone, never &quot;no expiration&quot;.</li><li><strong>Name it for its job</strong> (<code>week13-jules-roomly-fork</code>), so you can tell what to revoke.</li></ul><p><strong>In GitHub Actions,</strong> set the workflow&#39;s <code>permissions:</code> block to the minimum (<code>contents: read</code> by default) and grant write permissions per job. Workflows triggered by pull requests from forks must never get secrets.</p><p><strong>For MCP servers that reach GitHub,</strong> use a read-only token where the task allows it. A public issue once led an agent with a broad token to copy private-repository data into a public PR (<a href="./../resources/case-studies.html#a2-github-mcp-a-public-issue-leaks-a-private-repository">case study</a>); a read-only token removes the way out.</p><p><strong>Never give an agent</strong> a classic personal access token, your SSH key, a token with <code>admin</code> or <code>delete_repo</code> scope, or a cloud console credential.</p><h2 id="spending-caps-at-zero" tabindex="-1">Spending caps at zero <a class="header-anchor" href="#spending-caps-at-zero" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Spending caps at zero&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On every metered service, the cap is <strong>zero</strong> or the lowest value the service allows:</p><ul><li><strong>No payment method</strong> on any account used in the course. GitHub Codespaces and Actions, for example, are blocked rather than billed when the free quota runs out, if there&#39;s no card.</li><li><strong>Copilot:</strong> don&#39;t set a paid budget. When the allowance runs out, switch tools.</li><li><strong>Agentic workflows and cloud agents:</strong> never run one whose per-run cap could exceed a free allowance. GitHub Agentic Workflows&#39; default per-run cap is far larger than a student&#39;s monthly allowance; lower it before the first run (<a href="./../TOOLS.html#free-infrastructure">Tool Handout: Free infrastructure</a>; <a href="./safety-contract.html#6-spending-caps-at-zero">contract rule 6</a>).</li><li><strong>Department-funded credits</strong> (if your instructor provides them): a hard limit set by the department, per student, with no automatic top-up.</li></ul><h2 id="never-in-a-repository" tabindex="-1">Never in a repository <a class="header-anchor" href="#never-in-a-repository" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Never in a repository&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Before your first commit in any repository:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> check-ignore</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -v</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> .env</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> status</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --short</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Before every push from week 4 on, run the secret scan your kit or team fork provides (week 10 adds a history-aware scan), and keep GitHub&#39;s <strong>push protection</strong> on in your public forks (Settings → Code security). Push protection is free on public repositories and blocks pushes that contain known key formats, but it recognizes only formats it knows, so it is a backstop, not a plan.</p><p>Things that leak keys in practice: a debug <code>console.log(process.env)</code>, a trace file committed with request headers in it (<code>mini-agent</code>&#39;s trace must never record the key), a screenshot of a terminal, a pasted error message that includes the request, and a <code>.env.local</code> that <code>.gitignore</code> didn&#39;t match.</p><h2 id="if-a-key-leaks" tabindex="-1">If a key leaks <a class="header-anchor" href="#if-a-key-leaks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;If a key leaks&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Pasted into a chat, committed (even to a branch), shown on a shared screen or in a screenshot:</p><ol><li><strong>Revoke it now</strong>, at the provider, before anything else. Deleting the commit doesn&#39;t help: forks and clones keep history.</li><li><strong>Make a new key</strong> and put it only in <code>.env</code> or a Codespaces secret.</li><li><strong>Check usage</strong> on the provider&#39;s dashboard for anything you didn&#39;t do.</li><li><strong>Tell your instructor.</strong> Reporting quickly is always the right call, and on a free-tier key with no card the damage is usually nil.</li><li>If it reached a public repository, follow <a href="./../resources/troubleshooting.html#i-committed-a-secret">troubleshooting: I committed a secret</a> to clean the history, <em>after</em> revoking.</li></ol><p>Back to <a href="./">Week 0</a> · Current providers and limits: <a href="./../TOOLS.html">Tool Handout</a></p>`,40)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
